\chapter{An SSH server}
\label{ch:ssh}

Telnet sends every key, and so the password, over the network in clear text. \emph{SSH} (the Secure Shell, RFC 4251 to 4254) is
the same idea with cryptography: a terminal over a TCP connection, but the server proves who it is, and everything that follows is
encrypted and protected against changes. The SSH server of Ulix ([[sshd]], the file [[userland/sshd.c]]) is a sibling of the telnet server
([[telnetd]], chapter on servers): like it, it is a \emph{program} that the boot starts as a service, it holds the same terminal (the one on which a
[[login]] waits) through the pseudo terminal, and it is switched on and off with a line in [[/etc/rc.conf]], [[sshd_enable="YES"]] (the default; \emph{not} part of the system when it is
built with [[make TLS=0]], because it needs the same cryptography library as [[https]]: BearSSL). The first version of the server lived in the
kernel's network thread, like the first telnet server, and was moved out when the system had pipes, server sockets and a pseudo terminal; the last section says what changed.

\paragraph{What is implemented} is the smallest SSH-2 server that a current OpenSSH client talks to. Each step of the connection
has exactly one algorithm, so there is nothing to negotiate except to check that the client knows it:

\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{ll}
  key exchange        & [[curve25519-sha256]] (an elliptic curve Diffie--Hellman exchange, RFC 8731) \\
  server key          & [[ecdsa-sha2-nistp256]] (a signature with a P-256 key, RFC 5656) \\
  encryption          & [[aes128-ctr]] (AES in counter mode, RFC 4344) \\
  integrity           & [[hmac-sha2-256]] (RFC 6668) \\
  compression         & [[none]] \\
  user authentication & [[none]], see below \\
  channels            & one [[session]] with a terminal ([[pty-req]] is accepted and ignored) and a [[shell]]
\end{tabular}\end{center}

Ed25519 host keys are not possible (BearSSL has no Ed25519), and [[chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com]], the cipher that OpenSSH prefers, needs
the plain Poly1305 function, which BearSSL only offers inside its TLS construction; AES-CTR with HMAC is the older and simpler
design and every OpenSSH client still offers it. No [[exec]] requests (running one command: [[ssh host ls]]) and no port forwarding:
only the interactive shell.

\paragraph{Who is the user?} The \emph{server} is authenticated by the SSH layer: the client compares the host key with the one that
it knows ([[known_hosts]]). The \emph{user} is authenticated by [[login]], exactly as with telnet: the SSH layer accepts the method
[[none]], and the session starts at the prompt of [[login]] on the terminal, where the user name and the password are typed
(through the encrypted connection, which is the point). The password is checked by the existing code and nothing in the SSH
layer ever sees it in clear text. An SSH client that wants to send a password itself (RFC 4252, method [[password]]) is not
needed: a client that is refused the method [[password]] falls back on [[none]].

\paragraph{The host key} is a P-256 private key, 32 random bytes written as 64 hexadecimal digits into [[/etc/ssh/ssh_host_ecdsa_key]].
The key is made when the disk image is built
([[arm64/etc/ssh/]], by the [[Makefile]], from the random numbers of the host; it is not part of the repository, so every
image gets its own key), and the file is readable for root only: the server is started by the boot as root and reads it with [[open]]. The server prints its fingerprint at the start ([[SHA256:]] and 43 characters, the form that
[[ssh-keygen -l]] prints) on terminal 9, where daemons write, and [[sshd -k]] prints it on request, so that a person can compare it with what the client shows on the first connection.

\paragraph{One session, one terminal.} Telnet and SSH share one terminal, number 5 ([[TELNET_VT]]). The first one that comes in gets it
([[pty_attach]]), everybody else is refused until it hangs up. When the SSH client closes the connection, the programs on the terminal are ended
([[pty_hangup]]) and [[login]] asks for a name again. That is what keeps the next person from finding a shell of the
last one.

\paragraph{Where the code is.} Everything that touches BearSSL is in [[userland/sshcrypto.c]] (the same split as in the TLS chapter: BearSSL's
header files and the system's use different definitions of the same types, so one file sees one of them). The protocol is [[userland/sshd.c]], a state machine
that the main loop of the program calls again and again: it reads what has arrived, handles every complete packet, and sends
what the terminal has written. It waits for a second at the most, in [[tcp_recv]], and wakes up earlier when a packet comes or the terminal has output.

The cryptography runs in a process, on its normal stack. In the first version it ran in the kernel's network thread on a stack of 16 KByte, which was acceptable for a
teaching system but a limit that every change had to respect, and a bug in it could end the system. Now a bug in the SSH server ends the SSH server.

