\section{The program, the service and the system without TLS}
\label{src:sshd-service}

The file \texttt{userland/sshd.c} is the whole program: the chunks above followed by \texttt{main}. It is started like \texttt{telnetd}, by the
boot, as the service \texttt{sshd}. The description is the file \texttt{/etc/rc.d/sshd}: switched on unless \texttt{rc.conf} says \texttt{sshd\_enable="NO"}
(as before), and installed only in a system that was built with TLS, because there is no host key and no cryptography without it.

<<etc/rc.d/sshd>>=
# sshd: the SSH server (port 22, a login on terminal 5 like telnet, but encrypted); switched on unless rc.conf says sshd_enable="NO"
command="/bin/sshd"
rcvar="sshd_enable"
default="YES"
pidfile="/var/run/sshd.pid"
@

A system built with \texttt{make TLS=0} has the program \texttt{sshd} as well, because the programs in \texttt{/bin} are counted by a kernel test and
should be the same in both builds (the same was done for \texttt{https}), but it only says that there is no SSH server.

<<userland/sshd-off.c>>=
#include "../ulixlib.h"

int main (int argc, char **argv) {
  printf ("sshd: this system was built without TLS (make TLS=0): there is no SSH server\n");
  return 1;
}
@

\paragraph{The fingerprint.} The kernel used to print the fingerprint of the host key at the start, on the console. A daemon writes to terminal 9,
which nobody looks at, and the key can only be read by root. So the fingerprint has a command of its own: \texttt{sshd -k} prints
it and ends, like \texttt{ssh-keygen -l} does. A person who logs in for the first time over the network compares what the client shows with what
\texttt{sshd -k} says on the console. The test does the same.

\paragraph{What this changed compared with the kernel version.} The protocol code is the same, line by line, with four differences. The state machine used to be
called by the network thread and was never allowed to wait; now it is a process that waits for a second at the most in \texttt{tcp\_recv}, so it sleeps
while the line is idle and wakes as soon as a packet comes or the terminal has output. The network system calls take the place of the TCP functions
(\texttt{tcp\_recv}, \texttt{tcp\_send}, \texttt{tcp\_accept}), the terminal is held through the pseudo terminal (\texttt{pty\_attach}, \texttt{pty\_read}, \texttt{pty\_write}, \texttt{pty\_hangup}),
and the host key is read with \texttt{open} and \texttt{read} instead of the kernel's file reader. And the cryptography now runs on the stack of a normal process:
the program has the stack of any user program instead of the 16~KByte of a kernel thread, which was the limit the first version had to live with
(``\texttt{sshd} in kernel space is a shortcut'', as the first version of this chapter said). A bug in the protocol code now ends one process and not the system.
